n  literally that being said  it was still rhetorical hyperbole and  the same probably goes for the white  supremacist label as well in part  because of written house's own willing  association with the far-right proud  boys which invited people to make  reasonable commentary on his personal  views for example in january 2021  written house was photographed and  recorded in a mount pleasant bar being  serenaded by members of the proud boys a  self-described quote western chauvinist  group whom the fbi labeled in 2018 as an  extremist group with white nationalist  ties at the bar rittenhouse posed for  photos holding up the okay hand gesture  with the proud boy members that hand  gesture has been co-opted by white  supremacists maybe ironically maybe not  ironically it depends on who you ask but  rittenhouse and his lawyers have long  argued that written house does not  belong to any far-right groups and is  not racist britain house's lawyers  successfully excluded evidence from the  jury that any purported ties to the  proud boys and rittenhouse even told  tucker carlson after his acquittal that  he supports black lives matter and as  one pundit put it quote the perception  that written house is a racist may be  incorrect but it's not unreasonable if  someone had published an article saying  kyle rittenhouse is a member of a white  supremacist organization that is a  statement of fact and can be proven true  or untrue but if an article said he  posed with the proud boys and flashed a  white supremacist sign and as a white  supremacist that may be an incorrect  conclusion but it's a reasonable one it  may also qualify as an opinion and on  top of that since kyle rittenhouse is  probably a public figure at this point  unless rittenhouse can point to specific  news reports claiming he definitively  belongs to a white supremacist group or  group that calls for engaging in  domestic terrorism as opposed to  speculation that he personally holds  such views it's unlikely that written  house will succeed to sue anyone for  calling him a racist a white supremacist  or domestic terrorist whether you're  talking about  rachel maddow or joe biden it's probably  not going to work but then there's the  question of can written how sue the  mainstream media or politicians for  calling him a murderer now this was  going on a lot and the issue of whether  written house could sue pundits or  politicians for calling him a murderer  is probably closer than the prior two  questions  shortly after written house was  acquitted in november of 2021 whoopi  goldberg for example said on the view  that she still believed he was guilty  even all the excuses in the world  does not change the fact that three  people got shot to me it's murder i'm  sorry and britton house told tucker  carlson that people like whoopi goldberg  defamed him by calling him a murderer  even after being acquitted so the  question is is he right well to give  everyone's favorite answer it depends  but probably not now legally speaking  the jury found written house not guilty  and the only thing that you can surmise  as a result of that verdict is that the  prosecution didn't meet its burden that  there might have been reasonable doubts  the jury might also have believed that  britain house acted in self-defense but  you can't necessarily know exactly what  they meant from the fact that they found  him not guilty it isn't necessarily a  repudiation of the idea that he  committed murder though if i was  representing written house i would  probably point it as very strong  evidence that he did not in fact commit  murder especially under these  circumstances and the statement that  someone is a murderer or committed  murder could be a little bit more dicey  and as explained by first amendment  scholar and my former law professor  eugene volk if a statement that written  house is a murderer implies the speaker  knows undisclosed or unpublicized facts  that could implicate him that could  potentially be defamatory for example if  a pundit stated quote i had a  conversation with rittenhouse yesterday  and he told me a lot about what happened  the man is a murderer that could be an  actionable factual assertion in contrast  if the statement merely expresses the  speaker's opinion based on the public  facts that have been discussed and  publicly disclosed that's probably not  actionable so if a pundit were to say  i've watched a lot of coverage of the  case and the jury got it wrong the man  is a murderer that is likely a  non-actionable opinion so generally  speaking when people referred to ridden  house as a murderer it probably wouldn't  have been defamatory because people  wouldn't have any reason to believe that  the speakers had inside knowledge beyond  what had been reported in the news and  of course there was lots and lots of  news coverage now a similar case  happened when radio host bob lonsberry  referred to john giselle who had been  acquitted of negligent homicide after  shooting a man to death in a hunting  accident as a cold-blooded murderer on  air and asserting that the fatal  shooting quote could not have been an  accident the court reasoned that  lonsbury's statements were based on  facts widely reported by the media and  could not reasonably be interpreted by  listeners as being based on undisclosed  information and furthermore these  statements were in the words of the  court quote obviously intended to be  confrontational rather than factual and  thus there was non-actionable expression  of pure opinion and of course it goes  both ways tucker carlson accused a  public figure of a crime and was rescued  from defamation thanks to his own  rhetorical hyperbole as you may know in  2020 model and actress karen mcdougall  who allegedly was paid 150 thousand  dollars to keep quiet about an affair  with donald trump sued fox news for  defamation after carlson accused her on  his program of engaging in quote a  classic case of extortion a